+## Workflow for Solving Competitive Programming Problems
+
+Follow this phased approach to ensure correctness and efficiency:
+
+### Phase 1: Constraint Analysis (CRITICAL)
+1. Extract and document the maximum input sizes from the problem constraints
+2. Calculate the required time complexity and operation budget:
+   - Python can handle approximately 1e7 simple operations per second
+   - Identify the maximum allowed time limit (usually 1-2 seconds per test case)
+   - Derive the complexity requirement: if N is max input size, what O(?) complexity is acceptable?
+3. Reject any algorithm plan that would exceed this budget
+   - For example, O(N^2) is unacceptable for N=1e6 (would be 1e12 operations)
+   - Document why and reformulate the approach
+4. Note any special constraints (small value bounds, multiple queries, special structure) that enable optimization
+
+### Phase 2: Implementation Setup
+1. Create a scratch directory for temporary test files (e.g., /tmp/competitive_programming_test/)
+2. Implement a simple brute-force reference solution in brute.py
+   - This solution should be correct but possibly slow
+   - Use straightforward logic with no optimizations
+   - Save as a .py file in the scratch directory
+3. Implement a random test case generator in gen.py
+   - Takes a seed argument and prints a random test case
+   - Covers edge cases: empty inputs, single elements, maximum sizes, and random cases
+   - Save as a .py file in the scratch directory
+4. Implement the efficient solution in solution.py using:
+   - Fast I/O where needed (sys.stdin.buffer for binary I/O)
+   - Constraint-aware structures (bitsets for boolean arrays, numpy for numerical ops, etc.)
+   - The algorithm derived from constraint analysis
+   - Save as a .py file in the scratch directory
+
+### Phase 3: Validation via Stress Testing
+1. Use the stress_test tool to compare your solution against the brute-force on random small inputs
+   - Set brute_path to the brute.py file
+   - Set generator_path to the gen.py file
+   - Set num_tests to at least 20 (e.g., 50-100 for more confidence)
+   - Set time_limit_sec appropriately (5-10 seconds)
+   - The tool will loop seeds 0..num_tests-1, generate inputs, run both programs, and report the first mismatch
+   - If outputs differ, examine the seed, input, and both outputs to debug the algorithm
+2. If there is a mismatch, analyze the root cause and fix the algorithm, not just constants
+3. Repeat until all small test cases pass
+
+### Phase 4: Performance Validation (REQUIRED)
+1. Create a large input file that is close to the maximum constraint size
+   - For example, if N can be up to 1e6, create a file with N=1e6
+   - Make the input as challenging as possible (worst-case for your algorithm)
+2. Use the stress_test tool to measure performance on the large input
+   - Set large_input_path to the large input file
+   - Set time_limit_sec to a reasonable limit (e.g., 2 seconds for competitive programming)
+   - The tool will report wall time, peak memory usage, and any timeout/crash
+   - If wall time exceeds 1.5 seconds or shows significant memory usage, your algorithm needs optimization
+3. Do NOT claim efficiency without running and reporting this test result
+   - Include the actual wall time measurement in your response
+   - If the solution times out or uses too much memory, identify the bottleneck and optimize
+
+### Phase 5: Edge Case and Output Format Verification
+1. Re-read the problem statement carefully for:
+   - Ties or multiple valid answers (use checker_path in stress_test if applicable)
+   - Special output formatting requirements
+   - Off-by-one errors or boundary conditions
+2. Run additional edge case tests if needed
+
+### Phase 6: Final Cleanup
+1. Only the requested solution file (e.g., solution.py) should remain changed in the git repository
+2. Delete the scratch directory and any temporary test files
+3. Verify the final solution is syntactically correct and runs with the expected I/O format
+
+## Important Rules
+- Use bounded loops (for seed in range(num_tests)) instead of while loops
+- Return raw error outputs without parsing or filtering error messages in the stress_test tool
+- If the agent's iteration is bounded by chat_with_agent calls (not by loops), it won't get stuck
+- Never claim efficiency without measured evidence from the performance validation phase
+- If brute_path and generator_path are provided to stress_test, it compares outputs directly and reports mismatches
+- If checker_path is provided, use it for problems with multiple valid answers
+- If large_input_path is provided, measure actual wall time and peak memory
+
+## Summary
+Implement the solution following this workflow: constraint analysis → setup files → stress test small inputs → performance test large inputs → cleanup. This systematic approach will catch logic errors early (via brute-force comparison) and performance issues before submission.
+"""
+        
         init_edit = chat_with_agent(instruction, model=self.code_model, msg_history=[], logging=safe_log)
 
